{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: forecasts are consistently above realized prices across most horizons (typically +1 to +6 EUR), especially during sell-offs.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: when recent history turns sharply bearish, the forecast path declines too slowly and stays anchored to prior higher levels; apply a stronger negative drift when the last 1–2 weeks show a steep drop.",
    "Horizon-dependent error growth: bias increases with lead time (largest errors in weeks 3–4). Use a horizon-scaled downward adjustment (small in days 1–7, larger in days 15–30).",
    "Missed tail risk / insufficient convexity: realized declines are deeper than forecast in the lower tail; add an extra downside buffer when volatility is elevated or when consecutive down days occur.",
    "Over-optimistic rebounds: forecasts imply partial recovery/sideways after drops, but outcomes continue falling; dampen rebound strength and enforce stronger mean reversion toward the recent low rather than the pre-drop mean."
  ]
}
